@page "/profiles"

<PageTitle>Draft vs Submit</PageTitle>
<h1>Draft vs Submit profiles</h1>
<p>Two profiles, one validator: <em>Save draft</em> validates leniently (format rules only), <em>Submit</em> validates for completeness. Typing follows the submit side: once you engage Title, it says whatever would block a submit. A field you have never touched stays quiet however badly its rule is failing. Summary's input waits for Submit, so Summary shows no error until you press it, engaged or not.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "DraftedBrief.cs", "Profiles.razor", "Profiles.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: 60 characters max. A format rule in the Draft profile: enforced always, including while drafting.</li>
            <li><strong>Title</strong>: required. A completeness rule in the Submit profile: a draft save ignores it, and engaging the field is what puts it on screen.</li>
            <li><strong>Summary</strong>: required. Its own completeness rule in the same profile. Its input sets <code>WaitForSubmit="true"</code>, so engaging the field runs the rule without showing a failure. A failing value gets no message, no summary entry and no red border until you press Submit, while a passing one still turns green. From then on Summary answers your edits like Title, until <em>Reset</em> starts the wait again.</li>
            <li><strong>Required marks</strong>: the asterisk on each label is derived from the validator rather than written into the markup — <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code> asks what the Submit profile demands, so a rule moving between the two buckets moves the mark with it. The glyph is <code>aria-hidden</code> and the input carries <code>aria-required</code>, so a screen reader is told the field is required instead of being read a star.</li>
            <li><strong>Reset</strong>: calls <code>_form.ResetAsync()</code> — a named verb for returning the SAME model instance to pristine: touched/modified state, messages and <code>HasSubmitted</code> all clear, without swapping to a different object.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Before typing anything, read the two labels: both carry an asterisk, because both fields have a <code>NotEmpty()</code> rule in the Submit profile. Nothing on this page declares that — the mark is read from the validator.</li>
            <li>Type more than 60 characters into Title — the format error appears as you leave the field.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Save draft</em> — blocked: format rules run even for drafts.</li>
            <li>Shorten the title and <em>Save draft</em> again — it saves, even though Summary is empty and wearing a required mark. The mark reads the Submit profile; this button asks the Draft profile, which holds no presence rule at all.</li>
            <li>Now clear Title altogether and leave the field — "Title is required to submit" appears at once, with no submit anywhere: the live channel runs the submit profile's rules, and Title is a field you have engaged. Summary is failing its own required rule and says nothing: you have never touched it.</li>
            <li>Type something into Summary and leave the field: it turns green, because its rule has run. Clear it and leave again: no message, no summary entry, no red border. Summary's input waits for Submit, so engaging it is not enough.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Submit</em>: Summary speaks for the first time. Title's entry was already in the error summary, so the submit is what adds the second one. From here Summary answers your edits like Title: type a summary and leave the field, and its message clears with no second submit.</li>
            <li>Click any entry in the error summary — it focuses the offending field.</li>
            <li>Fill everything in and submit — the status line below confirms it.</li>
            <li>Type a 61-character title again and leave the field, so the format error is showing before you touch anything else, then click <em>Reset</em> — the error clears and the form returns to pristine, but the over-long title stays in the box: <code>ResetAsync()</code> never writes model properties.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_brief" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _brief.Title" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_brief.Title" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _brief.Title" /></div>
    <div class="field"><label>Summary <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _brief.Summary" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_brief.Summary" WaitForSubmit="true" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _brief.Summary" /></div>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
        <button type="button" @onclick="SaveDraft">Save draft</button>
        <button type="button" @onclick="Reset">Reset</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
